# GPS Tracker application options.

mainmenu "GPS Tracker"

config TRACKER_CLOUD_PROVIDER
	string "Telemetry cloud provider: \"nrfcloud\" or \"hologram\""
	default "nrfcloud"
	help
	  Where telemetry reports are published. Both providers send the same
	  payload from telemetry_build_json(), so the web app's schema is
	  unchanged either way - only the envelope around it and the backend's
	  polling endpoint differ.

	  "nrfcloud" is CoAP over DTLS and requires per-device credentials in the
	  modem key store; see the README for the one-time provisioning steps. It
	  is also the only provider that can serve A-GNSS assistance.

	  "hologram" is the Embedded Cloud Socket over plain TCP and needs an
	  8-character Data Engine device key, which Hologram does not issue for
	  Hyper-provisioned SIMs.

	  Changing this one line is the whole switch: every provider-specific
	  option below and in prj.conf stays valid either way.

config TRACKER_CLOUD_NRF
	bool
	default y if TRACKER_CLOUD_PROVIDER = "nrfcloud"

config TRACKER_CLOUD_HOLOGRAM
	bool
	default y if TRACKER_CLOUD_PROVIDER = "hologram"

config TRACKER_CLOUD_NRF_LIB
	bool
	default y
	select NRF_CLOUD
	select NRF_CLOUD_COAP
	select NRF_CLOUD_CHECK_CREDENTIALS
	select MODEM_JWT
	select MODEM_KEY_MGMT
	select DATE_TIME
	select NETWORKING
	select NET_SOCKETS
	select NET_SOCKETS_OFFLOAD
	select NET_IPV4
	help
	  Hidden, and deliberately unconditional: the nRF Cloud stack is pulled in
	  whichever provider is selected, so that every CONFIG_NRF_CLOUD_*,
	  CONFIG_COAP_* and CONFIG_DATE_TIME* line in prj.conf remains a valid
	  assignment. Zephyr aborts the build on a handwritten assignment that
	  does not take effect, so without this a "hologram" build would need its
	  own conf file. Only cloud_nrf.c or cloud_hologram.c is compiled (see
	  CMakeLists.txt), and --gc-sections drops the unreferenced library code
	  from a Hologram image.

config TRACKER_NRF_CLOUD_APP_ID
	string "appId reported in the nRF Cloud device message envelope"
	default "GPSTRACKER"
	help
	  Deliberately not "GNSS": nRF Cloud's built-in GNSS card expects
	  {lat,lon,acc,alt,spd,hdg}, which is not this tracker's schema. A custom
	  appId keeps our payload out of that card and lets the backend filter
	  /v1/messages by appId.

config TRACKER_NRF_CLOUD_PORTAL_LOCATION
	bool "Also send position in nRF Cloud's native format"
	help
	  Sends a second, standard GNSS message per report so the device shows up
	  on the nRF Cloud portal's map. Useful when debugging in the field
	  without the web app, at roughly double the messages and data.

config TRACKER_CLOUD_CONNECT_BUDGET_SECONDS
	int "Watchdog budget for a cloud connect or resume (seconds)"
	default 240
	help
	  A cold connect is a DTLS handshake (up to 123 s) plus an authentication
	  exchange, during which the main thread is blocked and cannot feed the
	  watchdog itself. Must exceed that worst case but stay under the
	  watchdog timeout.

config TRACKER_MODEM_TIME_WAIT_SECONDS
	int "Bounded wait for modem network time before the first connect (seconds)"
	default 60
	help
	  The JWT used to authenticate is signed by the modem, which needs network
	  time. On a cold boot that can lag registration by a few seconds.

config TRACKER_AGNSS
	bool "Fetch A-GNSS assistance from nRF Cloud"
	default y
	select NRF_CLOUD_AGNSS
	select MODEM_INFO
	select MODEM_INFO_ADD_NETWORK
	help
	  Downloads ephemerides, almanac, time and a coarse position and injects
	  them into the onboard GNSS. Requested proactively when the modem signals
	  it has no valid assistance (cold start), and as a fallback when a fix
	  attempt times out. Typically cuts time-to-first-fix from over a minute
	  to a few seconds, which mostly shows up as battery life.

	  Only ever applies to the onboard receiver, and only when the provider is
	  "nrfcloud" - both are runtime checks (see agnss_wanted() in main.c), so
	  this stays selectable regardless. With an external GNSS module doing the
	  acquiring it matters only on the fallback path.

# These two shape the main report loop rather than the assistance module, so
# they stay defined even when TRACKER_AGNSS is off - main.c references them
# unconditionally and lets the compiler drop the dead branches.

config TRACKER_AGNSS_PROACTIVE_WAIT_SECONDS
	int "How long to wait after GNSS start for an assistance request (seconds)"
	default 5
	help
	  The modem raises its request within a second or two of starting, and
	  only when it actually lacks data - so this doubles as a cold-start
	  test. A warm receiver never fires it and the wait costs nothing.
	  Ignored unless TRACKER_AGNSS is enabled.

config TRACKER_AGNSS_FIX_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
	int "Fix window after assistance was injected (seconds)"
	default 90
	help
	  Only used for the retry following a timed-out fix attempt. With valid
	  assistance a fix normally lands in seconds, so this is mostly a bound
	  on "the sky is genuinely not visible". Ignored unless TRACKER_AGNSS is
	  enabled.

if TRACKER_AGNSS

config TRACKER_AGNSS_BUDGET_SECONDS
	int "Watchdog budget for the assistance download and injection (seconds)"
	default 180

config TRACKER_AGNSS_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS
	int "Minimum interval between assistance downloads (seconds)"
	default 1800
	help
	  Assistance stays useful for hours, so refetching on every failed cycle
	  would just burn data. Guards against a covered antenna turning into a
	  download every nine minutes.

config TRACKER_AGNSS_STOP_GNSS_DURING_FETCH
	bool "Stop the GNSS receiver while downloading assistance"
	default y
	help
	  In LTE-M/GPS coexistence the modem time-shares one radio, so a searching
	  receiver slows the download that would have helped it. Injection is
	  still legal while stopped - it only needs GNSS enabled in the functional
	  mode, not running.

config TRACKER_AGNSS_FULL_REQUEST_FALLBACK
	bool "Request the full assistance set if the modem has not asked yet"
	default y
	help
	  nRF Cloud's CoAP API requires an explicit list of what to send, which
	  normally comes from the modem's own request. This synthesises a
	  request-everything frame for the case where assistance is wanted before
	  the modem has asked for any.

endif # TRACKER_AGNSS

config TRACKER_MOTION_WAKE
	bool "Wake from the battery sleep on a jolt (onboard accelerometer)"
	depends on DT_HAS_ST_LIS2DH_ENABLED
	default y
	help
	  Uses the LIS2DH's any-motion interrupt so a tow, an impact, or someone
	  getting in pulls the tracker out of its nine-minute sleep immediately
	  instead of going unreported until the next scheduled wake. The sensor
	  raises the interrupt itself, so nothing has to stay awake polling it.

	  On a jolt the tracker reports every TRACKER_CHARGING_INTERVAL_SECONDS -
	  the same cadence it uses on external power - until either power turns up
	  (it was you getting in the car, and normal powered operation just
	  continues) or TRACKER_MOTION_SURGE_SECONDS pass with no further movement.

	  Needs CONFIG_LIS2DH_TRIGGER_GLOBAL_THREAD=y and
	  CONFIG_LIS2DH_ACCEL_HP_FILTERS=y in prj.conf; those live in a Kconfig
	  choice and a driver menu that cannot be selected from here, so motion.c
	  fails the build with a pointer to them if they are missing.

if TRACKER_MOTION_WAKE

config TRACKER_MOTION_THRESHOLD_MG
	int "Jolt threshold (milli-g)"
	default 350
	range 16 2000
	help
	  How hard the tracker has to be shaken before it wakes. Applied to each
	  axis after a high-pass filter, so this is a change in acceleration, not
	  a total - gravity does not count towards it.

	  350 mg trips on a door slam, someone climbing in, or a tow hookup, but
	  not on wind or a passing truck. Lower catches gentle handling at the
	  cost of more false alarms, which are cheap here: applying power ends the
	  surge immediately. The upper bound is the 2 g full scale the driver is
	  configured for, and the hardware step is about 16 mg.

config TRACKER_MOTION_DURATION_SAMPLES
	int "Samples the threshold must be exceeded for"
	default 2
	range 0 127
	help
	  Debounce, counted in accelerometer samples, so the wall-clock time is
	  this divided by TRACKER_MOTION_ODR_HZ - 40 ms at the defaults. 0 fires on
	  the first sample over the line, which also means a single noisy sample
	  wakes the tracker; a couple of samples rejects those while a real jolt,
	  which rings for a good fraction of a second, still gets through.

	  Only useful if the ODR is high enough for a few samples to be a short
	  time. At 10 Hz the smallest debounce available is 100 ms, which is long
	  enough to miss a sharp impact entirely.

config TRACKER_MOTION_ODR_HZ
	int "Accelerometer output data rate while armed (Hz)"
	default 50
	help
	  The rate the sensor samples at: how quickly a jolt is noticed, how fine
	  the TRACKER_MOTION_DURATION_SAMPLES debounce can be, and how much current
	  is drawn across a nine-minute sleep. Must be one of the rates the LIS2DH
	  supports: 1, 10, 25, 50, 100, 200 or 400.

	  50 Hz costs single-digit microamps and puts five samples inside a 100 ms
	  event, which is what makes a debounce worth having. Dropping to 10 Hz
	  saves a little current but samples only every 100 ms, so short impacts
	  can fall between samples.

config TRACKER_MOTION_HP_CUTOFF
	int "High-pass filter cutoff selector (0-3)"
	default 2
	range 0 3
	help
	  The HPCF field of the LIS2DH's CTRL2. Per the datasheet the cutoff is a
	  fraction of the output data rate: 0 = ODR/50, 1 = ODR/100, 2 = ODR/200,
	  3 = ODR/400.

	  Because it tracks the ODR, raising TRACKER_MOTION_ODR_HZ also raises the
	  cutoff and filters out more slow movement. The default pairs with 50 Hz
	  to put the corner near 0.25 Hz, roughly where it sat at 10 Hz and
	  ODR/50 - low enough that gravity is still removed, high enough that a
	  slow push or rock of the car still registers.

	  Raise the selector (lower cutoff) if slow movement is being missed; lower
	  it if slow drift is causing false wakes.

endif # TRACKER_MOTION_WAKE

# These two shape the main report loop rather than the motion module, so they
# stay defined even when TRACKER_MOTION_WAKE is off - main.c references them
# unconditionally and lets the compiler drop the dead branches. Same reasoning
# as the two A-GNSS timings above.

config TRACKER_MOTION_SETTLE_CYCLES
	int "Battery sleeps to let pass after losing power before arming motion"
	default 1
	range 0 20
	help
	  Losing external power means the engine just stopped, which is the exact
	  moment someone is collecting their things and slamming doors. Arming the
	  jolt interrupt straight away turns every trip you take into a ten-minute
	  surge. This delays it by whole wake cycles instead.

	  0 arms on the first sleep after power is lost. 1 finishes the cycle,
	  sleeps TRACKER_SLEEP_SECONDS unarmed, wakes and reports as usual, and
	  arms before the sleep after that - so roughly nine minutes of grace,
	  which is comfortably longer than it takes to walk away from a car.

	  The count restarts whenever external power is seen again, so every
	  unplug gets the full delay. It does not restart on a reset: a tracker
	  that reboots while parked arms on its first sleep rather than leaving
	  the car unwatched for another cycle.

	  The cost is that the car is genuinely unwatched for that long after you
	  park. Set 0 if that matters more than the false surges.

config TRACKER_MOTION_SURGE_SECONDS
	int "How long frequent reporting continues after a jolt (seconds)"
	default 600
	help
	  Measured from the most recent jolt, not from the first, so a tow in
	  progress keeps reporting for as long as it is being moved while a
	  one-off false alarm times out on schedule.

	  Long enough to be useful if the car is being taken, short enough that a
	  false alarm does not cost much battery. Note there is no upper bound on
	  a surge that keeps being extended. Ignored unless TRACKER_MOTION_WAKE is
	  enabled.

config TRACKER_HW_REVISION
	string "Hardware revision reported in the telemetry \"hw\" field"
	default "feather-nrf9151"
	help
	  Identifies the board this firmware is running on, since nothing in the
	  image can work that out at runtime. Paired with the "fw" field, which
	  is the UTC build time stamped in by cmake/build_stamp.cmake, this lets
	  the backend tell later which combination produced a record - the field
	  the Dash-era data never had and had to be inferred from dates.

	  Sent verbatim on every report, so keep it short: telemetry_build_json()
	  is bounded at 256 bytes and a long string here eats that budget.

config TRACKER_WATCHDOG_MARGIN_SECONDS
	int "Watchdog margin above TRACKER_SLEEP_SECONDS (seconds)"
	default 300
	help
	  The hardware watchdog keeps counting through the battery sleep, so its
	  timeout is the sleep plus this margin. The margin must cover the whole
	  awake portion of a cycle: LTE registration, a cloud connect, both fix
	  windows and an assistance download.

config HOLOGRAM_DEVICE_KEY
	string "Hologram device key (8-character 'SIM Key')"
	default "CHANGEME"
	help
	  Per-device key used to authenticate to the Embedded Cloud Socket API.
	  On the dashboard: open the device, Webhooks tab, Webhook key section,
	  "Show key" next to SIM Key. It is not the numeric device ID shown under
	  Device Details, nor the account-wide REST API key.

config HOLOGRAM_HOST
	string "Hologram Cloud Socket hostname"
	default "cloudsocket.hologram.io"

config HOLOGRAM_PORT
	int "Hologram Cloud Socket TCP port"
	default 9999

config HOLOGRAM_TOPIC
	string "Message topic/tag reported to the Data Engine"
	default "TRACKER"

config TRACKER_SLEEP_SECONDS
	int "Sleep between reports when on battery (seconds)"
	default 540
	help
	  Matches the original ~9 minute battery sleep. The sleep is interruptible:
	  applying external power (VBUS) wakes early and enters the charging loop.

config TRACKER_CHARGING_INTERVAL_SECONDS
	int "Report interval while externally powered / charging (seconds)"
	default 60

config TRACKER_GNSS_FIX_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
	int "Maximum time to wait for an onboard GNSS fix (seconds)"
	default 120

config TRACKER_GNSS_EXTRA_FIX_SECONDS
	int "Extra time to keep GNSS running after first fix for better accuracy"
	default 5

config TRACKER_GNSS_EXTERNAL
	bool "Use an external NMEA GNSS module (Adafruit Ultimate GPS v3)"
	default y
	select GNSS
	select PM_DEVICE
	select GPIO
	help
	  Takes position from a UART GNSS module instead of the modem's own
	  receiver, because the onboard one does not acquire fast enough in
	  practice even with A-GNSS. The module must appear in devicetree as a
	  "gnss-nmea-generic" node labelled gnss_ext on an enabled UART - see
	  boards/circuitdojo_feather_nrf9151_ns.overlay.

	  The onboard receiver stays compiled in as a fallback, so this is not an
	  either/or: see TRACKER_GNSS_EXT_MODEM_FALLBACK.

if TRACKER_GNSS_EXTERNAL

config TRACKER_GNSS_EXT_POWER_PIN
	int "GPIO0 pin driving the GNSS module's power FET (high = powered)"
	default 13
	range 0 31
	help
	  The N-FET gate. Held low so the module draws nothing until a report
	  cycle needs a fix. This is a Kconfig value rather than a devicetree
	  gpios property - unlike every other pin on this board - purely so the
	  wiring can be changed in one place with the rest of the tracker's
	  settings.

	  Pins 1-8, 10-12, 19 and 23-25 are already taken by the board (i2c2,
	  button, spi3, PMIC interrupt, uart0, accelerometer interrupts, uart1
	  and pwm0). Set this to match your actual wiring before flashing.

config TRACKER_GNSS_EXT_WARMUP_MS
	int "Delay after powering the module before reading NMEA (milliseconds)"
	default 500
	help
	  The receiver needs a moment to boot and start emitting sentences.
	  Opening the UART earlier only gives the parser garbage to discard, and
	  the wait is paid once per wake cycle.

config TRACKER_GNSS_EXT_FIX_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
	int "Maximum time to wait for an external GNSS fix (seconds)"
	default 60
	help
	  Deliberately shorter than TRACKER_GNSS_FIX_TIMEOUT_SECONDS: with the
	  backup cell keeping ephemerides alive across the power cut, a warm start
	  lands in seconds. If it has not locked by the end of this window, the
	  onboard receiver plus assistance is a better bet than waiting longer.

config TRACKER_GNSS_EXT_UERE_METERS
	int "Assumed user-equivalent range error for the accuracy estimate (metres)"
	default 3
	help
	  NMEA carries no position accuracy figure, so it is approximated as HDOP
	  times this. Only used for the optional nRF Cloud portal location message,
	  which requires the field; the telemetry payload does not carry accuracy.

config TRACKER_GNSS_EXT_POWER_CYCLE
	bool "Cut module power between report cycles"
	default y
	help
	  Opens the FET for the duration of the battery sleep, which is where
	  nearly all of the module's ~25 mA would otherwise go. Safe to leave on
	  when the CR1220 backup cell is fitted: the module keeps its RTC and
	  ephemerides, so the next wake is a warm start rather than a ~34 s cold
	  one. Turn this off if there is no backup cell and cold starts are
	  costing more than the module's running current.

config TRACKER_GNSS_EXT_MODEM_FALLBACK
	bool "Fall back to the onboard receiver when the module does not lock"
	default y
	help
	  After TRACKER_GNSS_EXT_FIX_TIMEOUT_SECONDS with no fix, power the module
	  down and give the rest of the cycle to the modem's GNSS, with A-GNSS
	  assistance if the provider can serve it. Covers a disconnected antenna
	  or a dead module at the cost of a longer awake time on those cycles.

endif # TRACKER_GNSS_EXTERNAL

config TRACKER_LTE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
	int "Bounded LTE connection attempt timeout (seconds)"
	default 120
	help
	  On failure the tracker sleeps TRACKER_SLEEP_SECONDS and retries, rather
	  than blocking forever (mirrors the original 'sleep 9 min, try again').

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED
	bool "RGB status LED (red = no LTE, yellow = LTE only, green = LTE + fix)"
	default y
	select LED
	help
	  Uses the nPM1300 RGB LED to show connectivity state. Requires all three
	  LED channels in "host" mode (see the board overlay), which gives up the
	  PMIC's automatic error/charging indication on LED0/LED1.

if TRACKER_STATUS_LED

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED_RED_INDEX
	int "nPM1300 LED channel wired to the red element"
	default 0
	range 0 2

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED_GREEN_INDEX
	int "nPM1300 LED channel wired to the green element"
	default 1
	range 0 2

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED_BLUE_INDEX
	int "nPM1300 LED channel wired to the blue element"
	default 2
	range 0 2
	help
	  Not used by any status color; only forced off at startup.

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED_BLINK_MS
	int "Blink half-period while charging (milliseconds)"
	default 500
	range 50 10000
	help
	  Half-period of the symmetric blink shown while externally powered and
	  charging, so the default is a 1 Hz blink. Driven from the system
	  workqueue, since the PMIC LED sinks have no hardware blink.

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED_FLASH_ON_MS
	int "On time of the on-battery flash (milliseconds)"
	default 50
	range 20 10000

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED_FLASH_OFF_MS
	int "Off time of the on-battery flash (milliseconds)"
	default 1950
	range 100 60000
	help
	  Together with TRACKER_STATUS_LED_FLASH_ON_MS this is the "running on
	  battery" pattern - by default a 100 ms flash every 2 s. Deliberately a
	  low duty cycle rather than a symmetric blink: unlike the charging blink
	  this one is paid for out of the battery it is reporting on. A short
	  flash on a short period gives more chances to catch it while costing
	  less than a longer one on a longer period.

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED_FULL_PERCENT
	int "State of charge at or above which the battery counts as full (percent)"
	default 95
	range 50 100
	help
	  Only a solid LED means "externally powered and full", so this is what
	  separates solid from the charging blink. The percentage comes from
	  telemetry_battery_percent(), i.e. interpolated from battery voltage.

	  The PMIC's own charge-complete flag also counts as full when it is set,
	  but a threshold is wanted regardless: it is a definition the tracker
	  controls, rather than one that depends on whether a charge cycle
	  happened to run and terminate.

config TRACKER_STATUS_LED_ON_BATTERY
	bool "Keep flashing while sleeping on battery"
	help
	  By default the LED is blanked for the duration of a battery sleep and
	  only flashes while the tracker is awake. Enable this to carry the
	  on-battery flash through the sleep as a continuous sign of life.

	  The sink draws ~5 mA lit, so at the default 100 ms in 2 s that averages
	  ~0.25 mA - small in absolute terms but well above what the tracker
	  otherwise draws asleep, so it will dominate standby battery life.

endif # TRACKER_STATUS_LED

config TRACKER_FORCE_PUBLISH_WITHOUT_FIX
	bool "Report even when GNSS never gets a fix (position 0,0)"
	help
	  By default a cycle with no GNSS fix is skipped entirely. Enable this to
	  publish the telemetry anyway with coords [0, 0] and hdop 0, so the backend
	  still receives battery/signal/awake data (and a heartbeat) from a tracker
	  that cannot see the sky. Costs data and airtime on every fixless cycle.

config TRACKER_SKIP_LTE
	bool "Bench debug: skip LTE, run GNSS-only (no SIM needed)"
	help
	  Puts the modem in GNSS-only functional mode instead of connecting to LTE.
	  GNSS is modem-side and needs no network/SIM, so real fixes still work.

config TRACKER_OFFLINE_DEBUG
	bool "Bench debug: log the Hologram payload instead of sending it"
	help
	  hologram_send() logs the fully composed envelope JSON to the console and
	  returns success, instead of opening the socket. Lets the whole pipeline be
	  exercised on the bench without connectivity.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
